% M18-coverage-T9 (D-0270): a bits-arm line over a row with ORDER terms is written on the
% LADDER RULE, with no bridge pair and no order bound line. Its pol cites the bits terms'
% atom lines, the ONE bridge half each order fact needs (written at its first citation)
% and the row, so a declared order term's rungs stay in it; the clause is pinned by `ia`.
% k and m are on bits (default --bits-above 0); x1..x3 are held on the order encoding by
% their all_different. Every row mixes them: k >= 3*x1 + 1 (an order FACT pushes a bits
% claim), k + m + x2 <= 21 (bits facts and x2, at its root bound x2 >= 3, a wide order
% fact), m >= 3*x3 + 5 (a bits fact pushes an ORDER claim). Deciding x1 = 4 fails
% through the rows, so the trace is written under a decision over a satisfiable model.
% The root line `~k_ge_11 \/ ~m_ge_8 \/ ~x2_ge_3` is the first citation of x2 >= 3, so
% B1'(x2, 3) is written just before it. test_mutation.ml's ladder lanes corrupt that
% line's pol (its row dropped, an operand counted twice) and its `ia` (claim flipped,
% degree raised), and its bridge lanes flip the half's atom or shift its degree: each is
% refused, the pol's and the half's corruptions at the `ia` that cites them.
var 0..30: k :: output_var;
var 0..30: m :: output_var;
var 1..4: x1 :: output_var;
var 1..4: x2 :: output_var;
var 1..4: x3 :: output_var;
constraint int_lin_le([-1, 3], [k, x1], -1);
constraint int_lin_le([1, 1, 1], [k, m, x2], 21);
constraint int_lin_le([-1, 3], [m, x3], -5);
constraint int_lin_le([-1], [x2], -3);
constraint all_different_int([x1, x2, x3]);
solve :: int_search([x1, x2, x3, k, m], input_order, indomain_max, complete) satisfy;
